Subject: understanding C++ optimizations -- extra code runs faster
Message-ID: <ivKE20U0NaI.k5HeZDguMXAJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/ivKE20U0NaI/m/k5HeZDguMXAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I'm getting a very repeatable effect that
the method on var objects written as:

       double adjoint() const {
         if (is_constant())
           return 0.0;
         return adjoints[node_];
       }

is faster (by about 1.5% to 2.5%) than the version
without the test.

       double adjoint() const {
         return adjoints[node_];
       }

Any ideas as to what might be going on?  There are
a couple of other similar tests that have the same
behavior.  I'm baffled.

I'd always believed the old adage that "the fastest
code is the code that doesn't run at all".

I can't seem to reproduce the huge diff I thought I
saw with const vs. not in:

     inline var dot(const Eigen::Matrix<var,1,Eigen::Dynamic>& a,
                    const Eigen::Matrix<var,1,Eigen::Dynamic>& b);

It did just dawn on me in trolling through their code that
you can have different implementations for const and non-const
returns (either a ref or const ref, just like for std::vector).

- Bob

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Wed Jun 13 22:09:42 2012
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Wed, 13 Jun 2012 22:09:42 +0000
Subject: mixed parameter/data matrices and arrays
Message-ID: <YtuettU9TEI.0Gw7Wtgl104J@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/YtuettU9TEI/m/0Gw7Wtgl104J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

With the new design for agrad we're looking at, there's
the opportunity to mix parameters (stan::agrad::var)
and data (double) into a single matrix, vector, or array.

(Alas, this still won't let us do missing data a la
BUGS with NA entries.)

The issue is whether we need to do one check for is-var
or one for each item of each array.  For instance,
here's the way I implemented dot product:

     inline var dot(Eigen::Matrix<var,1,Eigen::Dynamic>& a,
                    Eigen::Matrix<var,1,Eigen::Dynamic>& b) {
       if (a.size() != b.size())
         throw std::domain_error("vectors a and b must be same size in
dot product");
       if (a.size() == 0)
         return var(0);

       double val(0.0);
       for (int i = 0; i < a.size(); ++i)
         val += a(i).val_ * b(i).val_;
       int node = alloc_node();
       for (int i = 0; i < a.size(); ++i) {
	if (a(i).is_var()) // ########################
           set_next_edge(a(i).node_, b(i).val_);
         if (b(i).is_var()) // ########################
           set_next_edge(b(i).node_, a(i).val_);
       }
       return var(val, node);
     }

The tests on the lines with the "#" signs are pretty
expensive (the routine's about 10% faster w/o them).
The is_var method itself is a simple lookup that gets
inlined (same speed calling function as brute-force unfolding).

We only need to do those tests once if the arrays are
all homogeneous.

The only place this comes into play is in missing data
problems where a parameter is half missing data and half
known data.  In the current Stan implementation, the
known data gets promoted to a var and everything's the same
type.

- Bob

